<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liugong Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liugong Island is a small island at the entrance to the harbour of Weihai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was the base of the Qing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a>, which was destroyed there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the Battle of Weihaiwei in 1895. Under the British lease of Weihaiwei from 1898 to 1930 it served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> base and summer anchorage for the China Station.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liugong-island.jpg" alt="Liugong Island" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Müller (Rolfmueller) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liugongisland_warmemorialhall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Liugong Island lies at the mouth of Weihai Bay on the north-eastern edge of the Shandong Peninsula, about 4 kilometres from the city of Weihai. It covers 3.15 square kilometres, with a maximum length of 4.08 kilometres from east to west. The land slopes from cliffs in the north to gentler ground in the south, rising to 153 metres at Qiding Mountain, and more than half of it is forest, mostly black pine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeology suggests the island was inhabited from at least the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and its name comes from members of the Liu family who settled there under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Under the Ming its people left for fear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, a rebel force led by Xian Wang took refuge there under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiajing-emperor">Jiajing Emperor</a>, and settlers were brought back late in the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a>, guarded by troops. The population was evacuated again in 1663 during an epidemic, and in 1690 three families, the Cong, Zou and Jiang, resettled it.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> the island became the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first modern navy, with a naval academy and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> centre. From 1887 more than 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> were set up around Weihai harbour and on Liugong and Ri islands, Admiral Ding Ruchang took command of the fleet on 14 September 1888 and made the island his residence, and an iron dock was built from 1889 to 1891. After the fleet's defeat Ding killed himself, and Japanese forces held the island from the spring of 1895 for about three years.</p><p>In 1898 the island passed to the United Kingdom with the rest of Weihaiwei. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> extended the old facilities and built residences, hospitals, churches, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> houses, a post office and naval cemeteries. After Weihaiwei returned to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1930 the navy kept the base under a further lease; Japanese troops landed on the island on 1 October 1940, and in 1949 it was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is reached only by boat. Its forest is protected as a national forestry park, and it holds naval training grounds, a small zoo, a museum of the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> and a memorial to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a>, the navy that has earned it the name of the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first modern navy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liugong Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It served as a summer anchorage for the Royal Navy's China Station, with a base on <b>Liugong</b> island.</blockquote><a href="/state/weihaiwei">Weihaiwei</a></li></ul></section>
